Quartic girth-six graphs with eighteen hexagons per edge, order at most 32
============================================================================

Fix an edge and its two disjoint depth-two Moore trees. Their 26 vertices
have eighteen edges between their two sets of nine leaves. There are no
other edges within the trees: same-side extra edges create a cycle of length
at most five; opposite-leaf edges are counted exactly by the root hexagons.
Each side therefore has nine missing incidences, eighteen in total.

Let Y be the complete complement of these 26 vertices, with t=|Y|<=6.
Every edge from the Moore vertices to Y uses one of those eighteen
incidences. Summing degrees in Y gives 2e(Y)=4t-18. Nonnegativity gives
5<=t<=6. Thus e(Y)=1 if t=5 and e(Y)=3 if t=6. A vertex of Y with no Moore
neighbour would have all four neighbours in Y, contradicting e(Y)<=3.
Consequently every vertex of Y is represented in the first partition of
missing leaf incidences. No additional vertex can appear later.

At t=5, Y is one edge and three isolated vertices. At t=6, Y is a forest
with three edges, hence one of 3K2; P3+K2+K1; P4+2K1; K1,3+2K1. This list
follows by partitioning three edges among its nontrivial tree components.
A cyclic graph with three edges would be a triangle, forbidden by girth six.

A complete bounded search can thus acquire the accepted matching-forest
cover of all root configurations, the accepted exact same-side incidence
partitions and partial bijections between their blocks, keep only five or
six resulting outside vertices, and complete their residual degrees by
edges among those existing outside vertices. At most three edges are
added. All completed graphs must be checked on every edge and matched by
literal adjacency-preserving bijections to the retained source witness.
This is a working reduction, not a reviewed exact-count certificate.
